WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.480 --> 00:00:04.200
Dear Fellow Scholars, this is Two Minute Papers
with Károly Zsolnai-Fehér.

00:00:04.200 --> 00:00:10.650
There are two main branches of efficient smoke
and fluid simulator programs: Eulerian and

00:00:10.650 --> 00:00:12.179
Lagrangian techniques.

00:00:12.179 --> 00:00:16.509
Before we dive into what these terms mean,
I'd like to note that we have closed captions

00:00:16.509 --> 00:00:21.369
available for this series that you can turn
on by clicking the cc button at the bottom

00:00:21.369 --> 00:00:22.500
of the player.

00:00:22.500 --> 00:00:27.919
With that out of the way, the Eulerian technique
means that we have a fixed grid, and the measurement

00:00:27.919 --> 00:00:33.649
happens in the gridpoints only. We have no
idea what happens between these gridpoints.

00:00:33.649 --> 00:00:38.640
It may sound counterintuitive at first because
it has no notion of particles at all.

00:00:38.640 --> 00:00:43.160
With the Lagrangian technique, we have particles
that move around in space, and we measure

00:00:43.160 --> 00:00:47.920
important quantities like velocity and pressure
with these particles.

00:00:47.920 --> 00:00:51.809
In short, Eulerian - grids, Lagrangian - particles.

00:00:51.809 --> 00:00:57.170
Normally, the problem with Eulerian simulations
is that we don't know what exactly happens

00:00:57.170 --> 00:01:02.940
between the gridpoints, causing information
to disappear in these regions. To alleviate

00:01:02.940 --> 00:01:08.340
this, they are usually combined with Lagrangian
techniques, because if we also track all these

00:01:08.340 --> 00:01:13.200
particles individually, we cannot lose any
of them. The drawback is, of course, that

00:01:13.200 --> 00:01:17.440
we need to simulate millions of particles,
which will take at least a few minutes for

00:01:17.440 --> 00:01:19.590
every frame we wish to compute.

00:01:19.590 --> 00:01:25.130
By formulating his famous equation, the Austrian
physicist Erwin Schrödinger won the Nobel

00:01:25.130 --> 00:01:31.630
prize in 1933. In case you're wondering, yes,
this is the guy who forgot to feed his cat.

00:01:31.630 --> 00:01:36.170
There is two important things you should know
about the Schrödinger equation: one is that

00:01:36.170 --> 00:01:42.290
it is used to describe how subatomic particles
behave in time, and two, it has absolutely

00:01:42.290 --> 00:01:45.880
nothing to do with large-scale fluid simulations
whatsoever.

00:01:45.880 --> 00:01:52.230
The point of this work is to reformulate Schödinger's
equation in a way that it tracks the density

00:01:52.230 --> 00:01:58.460
and the velocity of the fluid in time. This
way, it can be integrated in a purely grid-based,

00:01:58.460 --> 00:02:03.610
Eulerian fluid simulator, and we don't need
to track all these individual particles one

00:02:03.610 --> 00:02:10.500
by one, but we can still keep these fine,
small-scale details in a way that rivals Lagrangian

00:02:10.500 --> 00:02:13.230
simulations, but without the huge additional
costs.

00:02:13.230 --> 00:02:19.299
So, the idea is absolutely bonkers, just the
thought of doing this sounds so outlandish

00:02:19.299 --> 00:02:21.420
to me. And it works!

00:02:21.420 --> 00:02:26.969
Obstacles are also supported by this technique.
Many questions still remain, such as how to

00:02:26.969 --> 00:02:32.739
mix different fluid interfaces together, how
to model the forces between them. I do not

00:02:32.739 --> 00:02:37.249
have the prescience to see the limits of the
approach, but I am quite convinced that this

00:02:37.249 --> 00:02:42.599
direction holds a lot of promise for the future.
I cannot wait to play with the code and see

00:02:42.599 --> 00:02:48.090
some followup works on this! As always, everything
is linked in the video description box.

00:02:48.090 --> 00:02:53.709
The paper is not only absolutely beautifully
written, but it is also a really fun paper

00:02:53.709 --> 00:02:59.749
to read. As I read it, I really loved how
a jolt of epiphany ran through me. It is a

00:02:59.749 --> 00:03:05.319
fantastic feeling when a lightbulb lights
up in my mind as I suddenly get to understand

00:03:05.319 --> 00:03:10.629
something. I think it is the scientist's equivalent
of obtaining englightenment. May it happen

00:03:10.629 --> 00:03:13.670
to you Fellow Scholars often during your journeys!

00:03:13.670 --> 00:03:18.930
And I get to spend quite a bit of time every
day reading fine works like this. It's a good

00:03:18.930 --> 00:03:19.790
life.

00:03:19.790 --> 00:03:24.909
I'd like to give a quick shoutout to this
really cool website called Short Science,

00:03:24.909 --> 00:03:30.579
which is a collection of crowdsourced short
summaries for scientific papers. Really cool

00:03:30.579 --> 00:03:32.109
stuff, make sure to have a look!

00:03:32.109 --> 00:03:36.169
Thanks for watching, and for your generous
support, and I'll see you next time!

